        "messages": [
         {
          "author": "gideon",
          "minute": "11:26",
          "text": "The 685 section is fine as far as it goes, but one thing next to it did get settled and it isnt written down anywhere on this page, so basically: the working directory gets a ledger file. The name lives in TURN_LEDGER_FILENAME and the value is turn_ledger.json. There is nothing exotic inside it, it only holds the things we already derive off the log itself, nothing that isnt recoverable from there. The field people keep reading wrong is turns \u2014 it is the line count of the jsonl, and the seed line is counted like any other line, so it comes out as responses plus one. Not equal to responses. tbh i had it as equal for about a day before i actually opened the file and counted, which is why im putting it here rather than leaving it in my head. Whoever lands on this page looking for the 685 discussion will most likely also want to know where that file goes and what the number in it means."
         },
         {
          "author": "dermot",
          "minute": "16:40",
          "text": "yeah, that matches what i see on disk. the edge worth spelling out, since it is what makes the plus one look like a bug the first time you hit it: a run that has been set up and has produced nothing yet still reads turns 1, because the seed line is already written. so turns 1 with zero responses is the normal empty state, not a miscount.\n\nand none of this depends on how 685 comes out. the count is a reading of the log, so wherever the stopping criterion ends up being evaluated in the loop, the arithmetic in turn_ledger.json stays the same."
         }
        ],
