       },
       "invented": {
        "kind": "doc_comment",
        "date": "2025-06-24",
        "title": "resume behavior for auto batch mode: matching responses files to job records",
        "collection": "engineering",
        "sections": [
         {
          "heading": "why this page exists",
          "body": "auto batch mode is landing in v0.1.26 and the same question has come at me three separate times this week, twice in review and once in the sync: when a run is interrupted and then started again against the same working directory, how does it decide that the responses file sitting on disk actually belongs to the job record it just read back.\n\nso this is the write up. its not new design, most of it is what the code already does, i just dont think it was ever written down anywhere someone could find it. if you are debugging a resume that did something you didnt expect, start here."
         },
         {
          "heading": "what a restart actually sees",
          "body": "on restart, for each request file in the working directory, we look for two things:\n\n- **the job record** \u2014 the provider side batch id, when we submitted it, which request file it came from, and the request count we sent\n- **the responses file** \u2014 appended to as results come back down, one line per response\n\nthe important thing, and i think this is the part that surprises people, is that these two are written at different moments by different code paths. there is no transaction around them. a run that dies at the wrong second can leave a job record with no responses file, a responses file longer than the job record expects, or a responses file that was written by a completely different submission of the same request file.\n\nso we dont trust the pairing on the basis that the two files are in the same directory. that would be convenient, it just isnt evidence of anything."
         },
         {
          "heading": "pinning a responses file to its job record",
          "body": "there was a note on the PR that got shortened to \"a restart needs both\", which i think caused some confusion about what the two things are, so to be explicit: what pins a responses file to its job record is the response count and who wrote last. the count has to line up with what the job record says came back, and the last writer recorded on the file has to be the submission in that job record. one without the other doesnt settle it \u2014 a count can match by coincidence across two submissions of the same request file, and a writer id alone tells you nothing about wether the file is complete.\n\nthe consequence for the ledger is worth stating plainly, because it reads like a bug the first time you see it. a first write hasnt been compared against anything, so the ledger says created, not verified. there is no earlier count and no earlier writer to check it against, and we are not going to promote it to verified on the strength of having just written it ourselves. it becomes verified on the next pass, when there is actually something to compare.\n\nhonestly this trips people up more than any other part of resume, which is most of why im writing it here."
         },
         {
          "heading": "what the ledger reports, and what to do with each",
          "body": "the resume ledger is per request file. the states you will see:\n\n- **created** \u2014 responses file exists, written by this run, not yet compared. normal. no action.\n- **verified** \u2014 count and last writer both line up with the job record. normal, this is the resume path continuing.\n- **mismatched** \u2014 one or both dont line up. the run stops on that file rather than appending to it. we do not repair it automatically and we do not guess which of the two is stale.\n- **missing** \u2014 job record present, no responses file. the batch gets polled again from the provider side, nothing is lost.\n\non mismatched the operator decision is to either keep the responses file and drop the job record, or drop both and resubmit. we deliberately dont pick for you. appending to a file we cant account for is the one outcome that produces silently wrong output, so its the one thing the code refuses to do."
         },
         {
          "heading": "not in scope for v0.1.26",
          "body": "recording these so nobody re reads this page looking for them:\n\n- dedup across separate working directories. out of scope, and to be honest i dont have a proposal for it yet\n- automatic repair of a mismatched pair. deliberate, see above\n- rate limit detection on resume (207 / 233) \u2014 deferred past this release along with the rest of that work\n- cost accounting for the requests a resume re-submits (293) \u2014 unblocked but not prioritised here\n\nin any case, for 0.1.26 the shape is: verify with both signals, report honestly, stop rather than append when we cant account for a file. thats the best we can do without a real transaction across the two writes, and i dont think we want to build that this cycle."
         }
        ],
        "channel": "the comments under engineering/resume-behavior-for-auto-batch-mode-matching-responses-files-to-job-records.md",
        "pieces": [
         {
          "piece": "response count and who wrote last pin a responses file to its record",
          "carried_by": "dario 10:42"
         },
         {
          "piece": "nothing else about the file gets compared on load",
          "carried_by": "dario 10:42"
         },
         {
          "piece": "a first write has nothing to compare against at all",
          "carried_by": "dario 10:42"
         },
         {
          "piece": "what the ledger carries at a first write is created, never verified",
          "carried_by": "dario 10:42"
         },
         {
          "piece": "verified is only what a load returns when both of those matched",
          "carried_by": "dario 10:42"
         },
         {
          "piece": "created and verified are different claims, not two points on one scale",
          "carried_by": "dermot 14:05"
         }
        ],
        "messages": [
         {
          "author": "dario",
          "minute": "10:42",
          "text": "the line under recovery that says a restart needs both the response count and the writer id is correct as far as it goes, but i think it reads like those two are a general integrity check, and they arent. what they do is pin a responses file to its ledger record \u2014 same count, same last writer, so the record is pointing at the file it was written for. thats the whole of it. nothing else about the file gets compared on load. not the contents, not the ordering, not the timestamps. so a match tells you the pairing holds and it doesnt tell you anything about whether the file is otherwise sane.\n\nthe piece thats actually missing from the section is the first write. at that point theres no prior record on disk, so theres nothing to compare the pair against \u2014 not \"we skip the check\", theres genuinely nothing to check against. what the ledger carries there is created, and it stays created. it doesnt quietly become verified later because a while went by. verified is only ever what a load hands back when both the count and the writer matched, so its a result of reading, never something we write down at creation time.\n\nin any case that is the behaviour today, and honestly i dont think the page is wrong so much as compressed \u2014 but compressed here makes created look like a weaker grade of verified when the two come from completely different places."
         },
         {
          "author": "dermot",
          "minute": "14:05",
          "text": "yeah. if i had to guess at the shortest way to say it: the pair is an identity check, not an integrity one. it answers is this the file this record was written for, and nothing beyond that, which is exactly why a first write cant produce it \u2014 theres no earlier record to be identical to.\n\nand that makes created and verified two different claims rather than two points on a scale. created is what we asserted when we wrote it. verified is what a load confirmed on the way back in. the section heading calling it validation on restart is doing some of the damage, since validation invites people to read one as the incomplete version of the other."
         }
        ]
       },
