       "invented": {
        "channel": "the comments under engineering/ws-050-batch-mode-50-cost-async-batch-apis.md",
        "date": "2025-04-07",
        "messages": [
         {
          "author": "nils",
          "minute": "10:42",
          "text": "The cancellation section says the sweep confirmed behavior looked correct after, and for the provider side of it that is true. but there is one thing out of that sweep i never wrote down anywhere, and it belongs on this page rather than in my head.\n\nOne of the runs we killed to exercise 614 left its turn_ledger.json half written. Truncated mid-object \u2014 the process went away somewhere between the write and the flush. json.loads dies on it, which is correct behaviour as far as it goes, except that whatever picks the file up next is not expecting a killed run, it expects either a whole file or no file at all. So the thing surfaces as corrupt state rather than as an interrupted write, and the traceback tells you nothing about which of those you have.\n\nWhat saved us was the jsonl sitting beside it. that one was intact, because an append either lands as a whole line or doesnt land \u2014 there is no partial line to trip over. We read it, appended the one line that had gone missing, and the count went from three turns to four, which is what it should have been.\n\nlet me think about whether that generalises. i think it does, for anything we only ever append to. The json is rewritten whole on every update, and that rewrite is precisely the window a kill lands in, so it is the file that will always be the damaged one. That's worth documenting here even though 614 itself is closed, because the next person who cancels a batch job mid-flight will hit the same unparseable file and the recovery is not at all obvious from what they'll be looking at."
         },
         {
          "author": "dermot",
          "minute": "15:18",
          "text": "so if i have that right: the json is a full rewrite, so an interruption can catch it at any byte, and the jsonl only ever grows, so an interruption catches it cleanly between lines. that makes the two files unequally trustworthy after the exact same kill, which is not how anything above on this page treats them.\n\nthree to four is the part i would keep. the count is what told you the line you appended was the right one and the only one \u2014 without it you are guessing how much of the tail you lost and you'd never know if it were two.\n\nthat said, the sweep was watching the provider: whether cancel returned, whether the job actually stopped. it never looked at what the killed run left behind on disk. those are two separate questions and this page only answers the first one."
         }
        ],
