       "invented": {
        "kind": "doc_comment",
        "date": "2025-04-09",
        "title": "payload plan file: what the tests hold on to",
        "collection": "engineering",
        "sections": [
         {
          "heading": "why this page",
          "body": "the payload plan file is now written once per submission under WS-050. emil has written up what goes in it and when it gets written, after the job-ID scoping mess, and that page is the source of truth for the contents. this page is the other half: what the test suite actually asserts about that file, how the fixtures are made, and how we write it out so the diffs stay readable.\n\ni have spent most of this week looking at diffs of this file in test output, so i am writing down what i learned before it evaporates. anything here that contradicts emil's page, emil's page wins on contents and timing and i will fix this one."
         },
         {
          "heading": "how we write the file",
          "body": "serialization settings are fixed, please do not vary them per call site:\n\n- `sort_keys` on\n- `indent` 2\n- a trailing newline at the end of the file\n\nthe reason for sort_keys specifically is that the key order moved between two runs and every test diff was noise \u2014 pages of reordered keys with one real change buried in it. indent 2 and the trailing newline are the same argument in a smaller form, the file is read by people in review output and it should diff like a normal text file does.\n\nin any case, the writer is one function now and these are set there, so nothing should need to think about it. if you find a second place that serializes a plan, that is a bug, tell me."
         },
         {
          "heading": "what the suite asserts",
          "body": "the assertions are deliberately narrow, because the shape of this thing is still moving:\n\n- the file exists after a submission and is exactly one file per submission, not one per request\n- it parses, and the top level keys are the ones emil documented\n- the job ID scoping is correct, i.e. two submissions in the same run do not overwrite each other\n- byte-for-byte comparison against a stored fixture for the two golden cases\n\nwhat we do not assert: anything about the cost fields, and anything about ordering inside the request list. the cost side is entangled with the resumed-jobs offset that WS-050 marked out of scope, and i do not want a test in here that goes red when someone finally fixes that. honestly the request ordering one i am less sure about, i think it is stable, but i have not proven it so it is not asserted."
         },
         {
          "heading": "fixtures and regenerating them",
          "body": "the golden fixtures live next to the test module. regenerate with the env var the other suites use rather than editing them by hand, hand-edited fixtures have bitten us before in the cache tests.\n\nwhen you regenerate, read the diff before commiting it. that is the entire value of the byte-for-byte case \u2014 if the diff is bigger than the change you made, something moved that you did not intend to move. with sort_keys on, a clean run should give you two or three changed lines, not fifty."
         },
         {
          "heading": "open items",
          "body": "- schema version field: emil and i have not agreed whether the plan file carries one. either we version it now while there are two consumers, or we accept that we will rewrite every fixture the first time the shape changes. i lean toward versioning now but it is his call.\n- the byte-for-byte cases only cover openai and gemini. anthropic batch goes through the same writer as far as i can tell, but that is inference from reading the code, not a test.\n- no test covers what happens when the plan file cannot be written (disk full, bad perms). to be honest i do not know what we want the behaviour to be there \u2014 fail the submission, or log and continue. worth asking dermot since it touches release CI.\n\nnone of these are blocking the current work. best we can do this week is keep the diffs honest and revisit the versioning question when emil is back on WS-050."
         }
        ],
        "channel": "the comments under engineering/payload-plan-file-what-the-tests-hold-on-to.md",
        "messages": [
         {
          "author": "dario",
          "minute": "11:24",
          "text": "the section above describes what goes into the ledger but not how it gets written to disk, and that gap cost us most of a day, so putting it here until someone folds it into the text properly.\n\nturn_ledger.json goes out with sort_keys, indent 2, and a trailing newline at the end of the file. not one of the three, all three, and the same three anywhere else that produces a file of this shape.\n\nthe reason is not asthetics. we had two runs where the key order came out different between them and nothing else about the runs differed at all, and after that every test diff was noise \u2014 screens of reordered keys with maybe one real line somewhere in the middle, and honestly you stop reading them carefully around the third one, which is the actual damage. sorting makes a diff mean something again. indent 2 so its readable when you open the file rather than one long line, and the trailing newline because without it the last line runs into whatever gets written after and that shows up as a change too.\n\nin any case, if you are looking at this page because you are about to write the ledger from somewhere new: sorted, indent 2, newline at the end. thats the whole rule."
         },
         {
          "author": "konrad",
          "minute": "15:47",
          "text": "Right, and to make the two runs concrete since they are not named above \u2014 it was the pair from 04-02 and 04-03. I compared them by hand at the time and the only diference was ordering, the content was identical, so the noise is exactly what is described here and not something else wearing ordering as a disguise.\n\nThe trailing newline is the part people forget, off the top of my head it is the least likely of the three to survive a rewrite. Anyway I went and looked at the ledger fixtures that are already checked in, and they are sorted, indent 2, final newline present, so they match this rule as written and none of them need to be regenerated."
         }
        ],
